  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn unseasonbly-potent shortwave will track east across the\nMidwest and Ohio Valley tonight into Tuesday, associated with a \nbroad upper-level trough rotating east through the Upper Great \nLakes. At the surface, a warm front will lift northeast across \nthe area tonight, followed by a strong cold front Tuesday night.\nThe latest SPC SWODY2 outlook has much of the area in a slight \nrisk, with a small sliver across the southeast in an enhanced \nrisk for damaging wind gust potential. \n\nThe main concern with this system will be the potential for \nsevere storms, given the strong environmental shear profiles, \nthough a large amount of uncertainty remains on both coverage \nand timing. The latest hires ensemble guidance suggests a \nnocturnal convective complex may immediately follow the warm \nfront on the nose of a potent 700 mb shortwave Tuesday morning \nacross North- Central Ohio. Redevelopment of this complex of \nstorms is possible by late morning, (around 11 AM) generally \nalong and east of the I-71 corridor, exiting east into \nPennsylvania by roughly 2 PM. In this case, any additional \nsevere threat later in the afternoon or evening would be \nlimited. This scenario appears the more likely one, given the \noverall dynamics of this system. The favored storm mode with \nthis scenario would be a larger mesoscale convective complex \nwith a more widespread damaging wind threat and potentially a \nfew embedded tornadoes. \n\nA second, but lower likelihood, scenario would be that the \nnocturnal complex mostly dissipates by Tuesday morning, \nresulting in greater destabilization ahead of the cold front and\na slightly later initiation time for thunderstorms, roughly 2 \nPM, and exiting east of the area by 7 to 8 PM. This scenario \nwould favor more mixed storm modes such as line segments and \nsupercells given the slightly better shear/instability overlap\n(more of an all-hazards threat, including damaging wind gusts, a\nfew tornadoes, and large hail).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA much cooler air mass will enter the region behind the cold\nfront on Wednesday, with highs falling into the low 70s (and\nsome places upper 60s) on Wednesday, only marginally increasing into\nthe mid-70s by Thursday. By the end of the week into the\nweekend, a large upper-level ridge will develop across the\nSouthwest CONUS and expand eastward into the Midwest/Great\nLakes. Temperatures will return to near or slightly above normal\nin the mid to upper-80s by Saturday and Sunday.\n\nMainly dry weather is favored for this period, though shower and\nthunderstorm chances may begin to increase by late Sunday.",
